R CMD INSTALL: compile preflight (rzig's check mode names what is missing)

A package needs the toolchain when it has compiled code (src/) or a
configure script R runs: configure, or on Windows configure.ucrt or
configure.win (there R does not run a plain configure, it only warns).
rzig, R_HOME/bin/toolchain/zig-cc (gcc.exe on Windows), finds each
toolchain group's tools as a compile does, on every channel: its check
mode (--rzig-check, --rzig-check=fortran when src/'s top level has
Fortran sources, then --rzig-check=build-tools) exits non-zero after a
line saying what is missing and the group's text, which names how to
install it. Run it before the configure step and stop with that text,
instead of failing deep in configure or make. With no rzig at all (R
without its toolchain package), stop with one message naming the
package to install, from R_ZIG_TOOLCHAIN_HINT (the conda and wheel
builds set it). A user Makevars (their own compiler) or
R_ZIG_NO_PREFLIGHT skips the check; binary packages never reach it.
Only src/'s top level is looked at for Fortran sources, as R's default
OBJECTS rule compiles them (install.R's srcs <- dir(pattern = ...)): a
package whose Makevars builds Fortran from a subdirectory passes the
preflight and stops later, at zig-fc, with the same no-flang line and
the compilers' text. Not recursive, so that a vendored Fortran file
that is never built does not ask for flang (the pip channel has none).
feat-no-host-paths PLAN.md, phase T; feat-standalone-toolchain PLAN.md,
B26 and B35.

